<TopicPage slug="subjects/paramedicine/foundational/ems-systems-and-the-paramedic">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Paramedicine is emergency medicine delivered at the worst possible workplace: the roadside, in
    weather, with incomplete information. Its system view matters as much as its clinical skill —
    survival in cardiac arrest, for example, is a chain with citizen links (recognition, CPR) as
    strong as the paramedic's own.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the chain of survival, link by link" widget="equationStep" config={chainCfg} />

  <h2>The paramedic's decision environment</h2>
  <p>
    Hospital clinicians choose from a menu of tests; paramedics choose from an assessment. The
    primary survey — airway, breathing, circulation, disability, exposure — exists because it
    ranks threats by how fast they kill. That discipline, repeated under pressure, is the
    profession's core competence, and its legal frame (scope of practice, protocols, consent in
    emergencies) shapes every intervention.
  </p>

  <h2>Safety is a clinical skill</h2>
  <p>
    Roadside work kills providers: struck-by vehicles, violence, unstable scenes. Scene safety is
    therefore the first intervention taught — the anaesthetic phrase is "a dead paramedic saves no
    one". High-visibility clothing, apparatus positioning, and the willingness to stage until a
    scene is secured are all patient care, indirectly.
  </p>

  <h2>A profession with its own science</h2>
  <p>
    Out-of-hospital research has rewritten practice: backboards declined, tourniquets returned,
    CPR pushed toward depth and compression fraction, and whole-blood entering civilian use. The
    national curriculum (and its NREMT certification) tracks that evidence — paramedicine is not
    transport with siren; it is emergency care with a shorter hallway.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NREMT paramedic certification domains; National EMS Scope of Practice Model; AHA chain-of-survival guidelines.</p>
</TopicPage>
